#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Benchmarks ripcloc against tokei, scc and cloc, in the manner of ripgrep's benchsuite.

    bench/suite --dir <corpora> --download                  # about 7 GB
    bench/suite --dir <corpora> --raw <run>/raw.csv | tee <run>/summary
    bench/suite --dir <corpora> linux rust                  # some of the benchmarks
    bench/suite --markdown <run>/raw.csv                    # the results as Markdown tables
    bench/suite --list

Each corpus is pinned to a commit or release, so that a rerun counts the same files.

Every tool runs with its default settings, since that is what its users see. The tools disagree about which files are
code, so each result records the files and code lines that the tool counted, and a tool that is fast because it skipped
work shows it. The equal-work benchmarks count copies of corpora with only the files of languages that every tool
counts alike, with ripcloc's attribution and size cap and scc's complexity turned off.

The tools run in rounds, each tool once per round, and the tool that goes first moves along by one each round, so that
a slow drift of the machine favors none of them. The first rounds warm the page cache and are not recorded. On Linux the
suite pins itself, and so every tool, to the cores in `--cpus`.

Peak resident memory is measured in runs of its own through `/usr/bin/time`. A process spawned by the suite itself would
report the suite's memory too, since its peak includes the memory that it had before it started the tool.
"""

